{"questions":[{"question_id":"1lfnfekfz","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2009 Paper 1 Offline","year":2009,"examDate":2009,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"basics-of-organic-chemistry","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","topic":"acid-and-base-strength","type":"mcq","marks":3,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"pthe-correct-acidity-order-of-the-following-isp-pimag-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-i3irrrzeetmvrlbg","content":"<p>The correct acidity order of the following is</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"IIT-JEE 2009 Paper 1 Offline Chemistry - Basics of Organic Chemistry Question 14 English\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/1lfrmoczf/ce30b597-bce8-44da-94fb-87d010725d36/b47758b0-cd0e-11ed-8124-cf50c8498624/file-1lfrmoczg.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"(III) > (IV) > (II) > (I)"},{"identifier":"B","content":"(IV) > (III) > (I) > (II)"},{"identifier":"C","content":"(III) > (II) > (I) > (IV)"},{"identifier":"D","content":"(II) > (III) > (IV) > (I)"}],"correct_options":["A"],"explanation":"<p>We know that, carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols. Further, presence of electron withdrawing groups on the ring increases the acidic nature and electron releasing group decreases the acidic strength. So, structure III, due to the presence of carboxylic acid is the strongest acid <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.893ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5698.6 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(892,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2475.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3531.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"37\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-37\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5309.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.<br><br> In structure IV, the +I effect of alkyl group reduces the acidic strength <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.893ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5698.6 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(892,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2475.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3531.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"37\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-37\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5309.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> of the carboxylic acid. Structure III, due to the presence of electron withdrawing substituent (Cl), is more acidic than phenol <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.893ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5698.6 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D45D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D45D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(892,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(882,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D44E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D44E\"></use></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2475.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(3531.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"38\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-38\" transform=\"translate(1278,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(5309.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>. Phenol (structure I) is the weakest acid amongst all <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"11.949ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 5281.6 1000\" xmlns:x